icularly the more prestigious the  products get  cost of advertising or pr is built into  a lot of products  you may not realize it but when you're  going to buy  brand name items you're paying in in  large part for the packaging for the  the  the image development and enhancement  and continuance that's going on you're  paying for them to give away free  product over here to try to build a  market you're paying for them to  subsidize things over here sometimes  that don't actually benefit you  so the question i guess with that would  be from utilitarian perspective  is that practice of building in all  these sort of costs because of  advertising and pr  is that okay from utilitarian  perspective  now that's an interesting question and i  think there's two things to think about  with that  so one would be  just think about the product by itself  most brand name products are not  particularly  um  great in comparison to a generic  counterpart which tends to be a lot  cheaper  but the generic counterpart tends to  look generic right it doesn't have a lot  of  image appeal  and so if you look at it just on that  basis you know this can of corn versus  uh that can of corn that's coming from  green giant or you know from round well  roundies is more of a  generic one  um  well sells corn well i'll just say that  um pour them both out they look pretty  much the same you're going to get pretty  much the same amount of pleasure out of  eating them  same thing would go for shampoo  um  anything you could you could think of  do you really need to buy gas at this  station as opposed to that station  although gas prices aren't changing that  much  um  a lot of luxury goods really you're  paying in large part for  these sort of costs  and actually a lot of staple consumer  goods you are too  now  if you look at it just in terms of the  physical product  it's kind of dumb  from from a bentham utilitarian  perspective right  do people actually enjoy buying the  things that have the glossy covers and  tie in with their hopes dreams fears  all their other affections that that  advertising appeals to  um do people enjoy drinking bud because  of the you know this you know the  interesting commercials and stuff like  that or coors or pick any other sort of  um and i'm gonna offend some people and  saying this any sort of you know  low-grade craft beer  in doing so  yeah i think a lot of people actually do  buy those sort of products in part  because there's a secondary pleasure  that's involved with that very  advertisement or other forms of pr  that have built a kind of mystique  around the product  and if you're bent them  well that would you know that would  factor into the pleasures and pains  wouldn't it on the other hand if they  really screw up the advertising campaign  um that could work against them in that  respect too  closely related to this  at least structurally different pricing  tiers  this is kind of an interesting sort of  structural issue too  um think about airlines for example  if you're flying coach  now it's no fun these days to fly coach  because they've cut a lot of frills  along the way right to be to remain  fairly profitable even though some of  them are actually in bankruptcy  that one then um  if you're flying coach  you may not realize this but your flight  is in part being subsidized by the  people behind the curtains  so the people in business and in first  class they're paying a lot more for  their seats and they're getting some  amenities that you're not getting so you  know you might have to wait for a pillow  or pay for a pillow they get it you know  automatically supplied  you got to pay for your alcoholic drinks  they get them automatically supplied  if you actually look at what they're  getting for their dollar amount it's  kind of a dumb trade-off on their part  they're not actually maximizing their  pleasure  they are however whether they realize it  or not whether you realize it or not  they are maximizing the pleasure across  the plane  because  they're  paying for  services that are you know  really overpriced  is helping to subsidize the services  whatever they may be  in coach  so  is it okay then to charge some people  premiums  and you could think about this with you  know in terms of  hotels you could think of this in terms  of all sorts of other  other related  topics and  objects and things that we would we  would purchase  does it make sense to sort of distribute  costs and benefits in such a way that a  small amount of people overpay  and it helps out a larger amount of  people from a utilitarian perspective  that's almost a no-brainer  that one actually works out quite well  let's move on now to some you know more  i don't want to say substantive because  these are all substantive talk topics  but you know the sort of traditional  ethics topics like say whistleblowing  right  what is whistleblowing it's when you've  got some sort of practice or product  that actually maybe poses a threat to  some people or violate some people's  rights or you know  the  something is going on that ought not be  going on and it's being covered up it's  not being reported either through the  media or to the appropriate law  enforcement agencies or to the  regulatory agencies  and a whistleblower is the person who  actually says hey hey this is going on  over here i'm going to reveal this to  you  do you have an obligation from a  utilitarian perspective  if you see some sort of practice that's  going on  that is supposed to be reported and  nobody else is reporting it and they  know about it do you have an obligation  to report it  was a good question  you would have to look at how many  people are actually being affected  and bentham you know one of the famous  things that bentham said he called  rights nonsense on stilts why did he  call that why did he talk about it that  way because in his view a lot of times  the the needs of the entire community  were being overlooked by people focusing  on my individual rights over here my  individual rights  so i would think that bentham might  actually be inclined to say  that  there'd be some cases where you  shouldn't blow the whistle  if only a few people are actually being  negatively affected by it  and  disclosure is not really going to bother  too many people  then  don't worry about it if on the other  hand there's a great threat that would  actually put a lot of people's  you know pleasures and pains into into  play  or actually are  currently affecting people negatively  then you would actually have an  obligation to blow the whistle from a  utilitarian perspective  another related topic that you could ask  about which i'm not going to go into  should we be protecting whistleblowers  should we be encouraging whistleblowers  and that's a whole different can of  worms right there  closely related unsafe products  now from a utilitarian perspective  unsafe products  again pretty simple  they're going to be going against the  greatest happiness principle right  because if it's an unsafe product and  it's being widely  promoted widely produced widely  sold  then a lot of people's  lives perhaps  a lot of people's health a lot of  people's um livelihoods pick whatever  you like are being threatened and you're  you're not minimizing pain you're  actually producing a lot of pain and  you're probably interfering with a lot  of people's pleasure as well  this is one of those classic cases where  it's a you know limited benevolence or  beneficence uh really should not get in  the way of universal or general  beneficence  now one thing that you could think about  though  unsafe products  we generally think about that in terms  of the consumer right the people who are  actually um  buying the product so  don't buy uh for example  really a lot of things that come from  china you know  they've got a pretty bad track record  for for environmental um uh  and health um  checking you know and concerns and  things like that  um  it's not a good idea to buy products  that you think might be tainted  and it's not a good idea to be selling  products that are that are tainted  either people do this because they want  they want to gain  something in the process  there's two other  holders who could be affected though  one would be the workers themselves what  we call the supply chain who are  providing these things so if you think  about apple for example apple got  themselves in trouble  in part because it got revealed that  some of these factories that the parts  are being assembled in are coming from  are kind of unsafe the products  themselves are not you know unsafe  they're not blowing up in people's  pockets  when they have them but the workers  themselves the people you know  closer in on the supply end are are  actually being negatively affected the  other thing would be unsafe say within  the larger environment  and this could come about two ways it  could be the production process itself  is unsafe because it produces say  environmental contamination  or it could be that the product that  you're generating is unsafe not  necessarily for the the consumer  themselves but for the other people in  the community who are stuck with you  know this person's products  another thing to think about would be  and then now we're changing um speed  quite a bit applying rules and  procedures very strictly  once you have rules you know do you you  keep them in place and say look here's  the rules everyone's going to be on the  same  level  or do you have some leniency  some discretion in them to allow for  cases so that you might actually you  know locally  work things out so as to minimize pain  and maximize pleasure  this is a real issue uh in terms of say  housing  right people have to live with each  other and we are still you know deep in  a problem with you know overvalued real  estate that then took a dive  people who were lent all sorts of money  who probably really couldn't have  qualified for that sort of money uh in  part because of predatory lending in  part because of you know foolish  government uh interventions that were  motivated by both political parties  uh just in different ways a lot of  different things going on there  think about foreclosures and actually  think about mortgages in general too  i remember years ago listening to a  radio show and this reflected my point  of view as well  where this is just before the bubble  burst um  the radio show host is saying  man you know when i had to qualify for  my mortgage i had to jump through all  these hoops  because you know risk was being  distributed through the system  i i had to you know actually do all  these sorts of things and i was worried  i wasn't going to get get my mortgage  and i had to save so that i actually  could put a down payment down and look  at all these other people that are being  granted them just like you know like  clockwork for some sort of you know  economic or political reason  um what's going on there and he was very  upset and you know that that being upset  that's a that's a pain that needs to be  thought of  um  what about the people whose lives are  made easier by allowing them easy access  to the funds that allowed them then to  buy the houses i guess for a short time  you know so long as everything keeps  going on pretty well they're doing okay  and that that actually maximizes  pleasure for them so maybe we should  ease up on it  now we're in the up with the opposite uh  condition where it's uh worries about  foreclosures should we foreclose on all  these people's homes  should we  help them out  you know should we  use government money to to fund efforts  to get some people out from being as  they say under water or should we say  well you made your bed uh now you have  to be responsible and lying  that would be something that we could do  a cost benefit analysis with from the  utilitarian perspective  two other things i want 